 ## Overview
-This method projects a 3D Gaussian primitive, defined by a mean and a covariance matrix in world space, onto the 2D image plane to create a screen-space Gaussian splat. It solves the problem of rendering millions of 3D Gaussians efficiently by avoiding ray marching; instead, it approximates the non-linear perspective projection with a local affine transformation, preserving the Gaussian form as a 2D ellipse [S1, S3, S4, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from naive point-splatting by accounting for anisotropic spread and orientation, and from exact perspective projection by using a first-order Taylor expansion (Jacobian) to maintain computational tractability and differentiability [S3, S5, unofficial].
